> It is a small step from "== true" comparisons to
> 	if (b==true) return true;
> 	else return false;

Depends on the variable type of "b"; if it is a
_Bool already, returning it undecorated is fine, if
not, the coder should do:

  return ((_Bool) b);

which makes things visually obvious to the next
maintainer that a cast is happening, thus this is a
place where things can go wrong if "b" later becomes
a different variable type.

> Truth values are truth values and should be
> treated as such; there is no need to convert a
> truth value to a truth value by comparing it with
> a truth value.

Well, except that ANSI C 1999 made a huge attempt to
move away from C's maintainability-destroying
"everything is potentially a boolean value" by
providing a specific type "_Bool", and the result of
that is that in modern C, treating anything but a
"_Bool" as a boolean value is strongly deprecated,
thus is bad coding style.

So, your guidance above is exactly contrary to
current practice and the intent of the standard's
upgrade of the language to include an explicit
boolean type.  Depending on readability, either
an explicit comparision, or an explicit cast, is
exactly what is preferred.

> The C convention of not using an explicit boolean
> type for truth values can lead to confusion, but
> "== true" comparisons are not the solution.

Given a choice between a cast and a comparision to
a boolean constant, I find the constant comparision
much more readable. You are permitted to hate it. I
don't have to maintain your code, and since I mostly
code in Java these days where much of C's sloppiness
has been repaired, you won't be having to maintain
my code either, if you stick to C programming.

